> Duplicates, radii and numerical range

Original identities distinguish duplicate points, and k-nearest retains their
multiplicity. Its heap orders by widened distance then original id and returns
nearest first, clamping k to tree size. Radius queries include the boundary;
negative or NaN radii reject, while positive infinity includes all points. An
empty tree has no nearest and throws, but k-nearest/radius return empty after
validating the query.

The subnormal and extreme controls below expose old square-distance failures:
raw double squares could make both candidate distances zero or infinity. Widened
hypot distinguishes these particular inputs. That improvement is not a proof of
exact ordering for every nearly tied binary64 configuration. Extreme point
clouds need not have representable padded plotting spans, so only the ordinary
duplicate-tie cloud is drawn.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kdtree_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/kdtree_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <limits>
#include <numbers>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::print("| control | actual nearest original id | stored count |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    const double d =
        std::numeric_limits<double>::denorm_min(), m =
        std::numeric_limits<double>::max();
    const auto show =
        [](const char *name, const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> & p,
           dm::geometry::Point2D q) {
        const dm::geometry::KDTree2D tree(p);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", name,
                   tree.nearest_trace(q).original_index, tree.size());
    };
    show("duplicate/equal-distance tie", {{2, 0}, {0, 0}, {2, 0}}, {1, 0});
    show("subnormal distances", {{3 * d, 0}, {d, 0}}, {0, 0});
    show("extreme distances", {{m, 0}, {-m / 2, 0}}, {0, 0});
    const dm::geometry::KDTree2D tree({{2, 0}, {0, 0}, {2, 0}});
    std::print("\n| query control | actual returned count |\n| --- | ---: |\n| k zero | {} |\n| k exceeds " "size | {} |\n| inclusive radius one | {} |\n| infinite radius | {} |\n",
               tree.k_nearest({1, 0}, 0).size(),
               tree.k_nearest({1, 0}, 9).size(),
               tree.points_in_radius({1, 0}, 1).size(),
               tree.points_in_radius({1, 0},
                                     std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity()).size());
    bool bad = false;
    try {
        (void) tree.points_in_radius({1, 0}, -1);
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument &) {
        bad = true;
    }
    if (!bad)
        throw std::runtime_error("negative radius");
    std::print("\nNegative radius rejects.\n");
    dm::geometry::kd_trace_plot(tree.nearest_trace({1, 0})).title(
        "Original identity resolves equal-distance ties").save_svg(
        "build/kdtree_controls.svg");
}
```
