| infinite radius | 3 |

Negative radius rejects.
\image(figures/kdtree_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A deterministic tie uses original identity rather than tree encounter order.)
\alttext(Two stored coordinates include a duplicate at the right; the table distinguishes identities and multiplicity that overlapping spatial markers cannot show.)

> Measure complete controlled query sets

For each nested generated cloud, the following program issues all32 queries on
an eight-unit circle around (10,10). It counts actual visited nodes and pruned
subtrees from each producing trace. A complete scan would evaluate n point
distances per query; that n column is an explicit baseline formula, not a
separately timed implementation. Trace copying of the whole cloud is outside the
query counters and adds linear overhead, so these figures are not timing
comparisons.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kdtree_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/kdtree_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <limits>
#include <numbers>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, mean, scan;
    std::print("| stored points | complete queries | mean actual visits | maximum visits | mean pruned " "subtrees | full-scan distance formula |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t n = 32; n <= 8192; n *= 4) {
        const dm::geometry::KDTree2D tree(dm::geometry::kd_demo_points(n));
        std::size_t visits = 0, maximum = 0, pruned = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 32; ++i) {
            const double angle = 2 * std::numbers::pi * i / 32;
            const auto t =
                tree.nearest_trace({10 + 8 * std::cos(angle),
                                    10 + 8 * std::sin(angle)});
            visits += t.work.visited_nodes;
            maximum = std::max(maximum, t.work.visited_nodes);
            pruned += t.work.pruned_subtrees;
        }
        const double average = visits / 32.;
        std::print("| {} | 32 | {:.6f} | {} | {:.6f} | {} |\n", n, average,
                   maximum, pruned / 32., n);
        x.push_back(std::log2(n));
        mean.push_back(std::log2(average));
        scan.push_back(std::log2(n));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, mean, "visits", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, mean, "",
                                                       {37, 99, 235}, 4).line(
        x, scan, "scan n", {220, 38, 38}, 2).title("Actual controlled query work, excluding capture copies").x_label("log2(stored points)").y_label("log2(mean visits or scan formula)").y_limits(1, 15).save_svg("build/kdtree_work.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| stored points | complete queries | mean actual visits | maximum visits | mean pruned subtrees | full-scan distance formula |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 32 | 32 | 7.843750 | 14 | 3.843750 | 32 |
| 128 | 32 | 11.968750 | 18 | 6.343750 | 128 |
| 512 | 32 | 15.781250 | 27 | 9.531250 | 512 |
| 2048 | 32 | 17.750000 | 28 | 11.750000 | 2048 |
| 8192 | 32 | 18.343750 | 29 | 13.156250 | 8192 |
\image(figures/kdtree_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Measured visited-node counts are separated from the full-scan formula.)
\alttext(The blue curve is the average actual node visits over32 complete queries per cloud; red is the n-distance full-scan baseline, not elapsed speed.)

The tree owns its coordinates after construction. The legacy by-value
constructor copy occurs before validation, while the new optional million-point
cap is checked before internal indexed records/nodes are allocated. Zero
disables that optional cap. Scalar nearest uses logarithmic recursion storage
without history allocation, but can perform linear node work. K-nearest adds a
bounded heap; radius output is owned and unordered by distance. Const queries
have no shared mutable state.

Nearest traces additionally own the original cloud and actual point/far-plane
records without replay; they remain usable after tree destruction. Their default
actual-record cap is one million, zero disables it, and admission precedes the
corresponding point distance or plane decision. Vector capacity, bytes, complete
cloud-copy cost and peak RAM are outside that record count. Plotting uses a
separate complete display-point cap and padded-span domain.

Independent Fraction squared-distance ordering checked 100 queries on integer
clouds using the represented binary64 query coordinates. All 1,789 actual
point/plane records passed distance, pruning and work-count checks. Another 100
ordinary unique-neighbor queries, including five-nearest outputs, matched the
frozen original. Native tests cover complete grid scans, duplicate identities,
radius boundaries and the documented underflow/overflow corrections; exact-real
guarantees for arbitrarily close extreme inputs are not inferred. The shared
ten-report build passed 131 native tests, 38 directly instrumented
address/undefined-behaviour sanitizer tests, 89 compiled public examples,
documentation coverage for all 388 scoped definitions and ten representative
clangd hovers. All four complete C++ report programs executed successfully and
their native figures were visually reviewed. Batch receipts are retained under
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-174-183/`.

> Reference

Jon Louis Bentley (1975), “Multidimensional binary search trees used for
associative searching”, Communications of the ACM18(9),509–517,
[doi:10.1145/361002.361007|https://doi.org/10.1145/361002.361007]. This
implementation fixes a balanced planar median build, widened rounded metrics and
deterministic original-index ties rather than promising a distribution-free
logarithmic nearest query.
